  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Pine needle split, caused by the fungus Lophodermium pinastri, is a foliage disease that affects various pine species throughout New England. This pathogen causes needle spotting and premature defoliation, particularly impacting young trees and nursery stock.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Black fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Look for small black, elongated fruiting structures (ascomata) that split open along the needle surface, releasing spores",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow-brown spots",
      "description": "Infected needles develop yellow-brown discoloration that may eventually turn reddish-brown before dropping",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Needle loss pattern",
      "description": "Infected needles often drop prematurely, starting from the lower crown and progressing upward on the tree",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This fungus thrives in cool, moist environments typical of New England forests and is most problematic in dense plantings or areas with high humidity and poor air circulation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'split' comes from the characteristic way the fruiting bodies rupture along a line (a slit-like opening) on the needle surface to release spores, making it easily recognizable to trained foresters.",
